"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,431\nThe life of a memory\n\n4\n00:00:12,231 --> 00:00:13,892\nPicture a US penny.\n\n5\n00:00:14,262 --> 00:00:15,895\nYou have handled thousands.\n\n6\n00:00:16,163 --> 00:00:17,974\nWhich way does Lincoln face?\n\n7\n00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:19,884\nWhat words are above his head?\n\n8\n00:00:20,261 --> 00:00:21,344\nIn a classic study,\n\n9\n00:00:21,364 --> 00:00:25,297\nmost people could not pick the real penny out of fifteen drawings.\n\n10\n00:00:25,681 --> 00:00:29,567\nSeeing something thousands of times is not the same as storing it.\n\n11\n00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:33,552\nThat is where this series begins: with the life of a single memory.\n\n12\n00:00:34,005 --> 00:00:35,684\nFirst it has to be encoded,\n\n13\n00:00:35,787 --> 00:00:37,522\ntaken in and given a form.\n\n14\n00:00:37,922 --> 00:00:39,827\nThen it has to be consolidated,\n\n15\n00:00:39,976 --> 00:00:42,153\nstabilized so that it lasts.\n\n16\n00:00:42,596 --> 00:00:44,646\nFinally it has to be retrieved,\n\n17\n00:00:44,729 --> 00:00:46,286\nfound again when it is needed.\n\n18\n00:00:46,694 --> 00:00:49,801\nA memory can fail at any of these three stages,\n\n19\n00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:51,665\nand each failure looks different.\n\n20\n00:00:51,835 --> 00:00:53,903\nThe penny is an encoding failure.\n\n21\n00:00:54,317 --> 00:00:57,170\nNobody needs those details to spend a coin,\n\n22\n00:00:57,269 --> 00:00:59,259\nso they were never really taken in.\n\n23\n00:00:59,771 --> 00:01:01,905\nRepetition alone did not do it.\n\n24\n00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:06,099\nWhat gets stored depends on what the learner does with the material,\n\n25\n00:01:06,352 --> 00:01:09,068\nnot on how often it passes in front of them.\n\n26\n00:01:09,238 --> 00:01:11,406\nConsolidation failure looks different.\n\n27\n00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:14,956\nHermann Ebbinghaus learned lists of nonsense syllables,\n\n28\n00:01:15,152 --> 00:01:18,209\nthen measured how much faster he could relearn them later.\n\n29\n00:01:18,675 --> 00:01:22,756\nAfter twenty minutes, he saved fifty eight percent of the original effort.\n\n30\n00:01:23,196 --> 00:01:25,205\nAfter a day, about a third.\n\n31\n00:01:25,522 --> 00:01:27,321\nAfter a month, about a fifth.\n\n32\n00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:29,375\nMost of the loss came early,\n\n33\n00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:31,164\nwhile the memory was still new.\n\n34\n00:01:31,334 --> 00:01:33,553\nRetrieval failure looks different again.\n\n35\n00:01:33,915 --> 00:01:35,399\nIn a classic experiment,\n\n36\n00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:38,380\npeople studied word lists sorted into categories.\n\n37\n00:01:38,765 --> 00:01:40,133\nAsked to recall freely,\n\n38\n00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:41,692\nthey missed many words.\n\n39\n00:01:42,011 --> 00:01:44,018\nGiven the category names as cues,\n\n40\n00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:45,595\nthey recalled far more.\n\n41\n00:01:46,048 --> 00:01:48,033\nThe words were stored the whole time.\n\n42\n00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:50,823\nThey just could not be reached without a way in.\n\n43\n00:01:50,993 --> 00:01:52,017\nOne caution.\n\n44\n00:01:52,449 --> 00:01:54,459\nThese three stages are a model,\n\n45\n00:01:54,479 --> 00:01:56,241\nnot three separate boxes.\n\n46\n00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:58,940\nRetrieving a memory also changes it,\n\n47\n00:01:59,155 --> 00:02:01,849\nand encoding builds on what is already stored.\n\n48\n00:02:02,434 --> 00:02:05,885\nThe stages overlap, and this series will show you where.\n\n49\n00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:06,848\nFor teaching.\n\n50\n00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:10,299\nWhen a student says they studied but could not remember,\n\n51\n00:02:10,319 --> 00:02:11,738\nask which stage failed.\n\n52\n00:02:12,219 --> 00:02:14,998\nDid they really work with the material the first time?\n\n53\n00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:17,642\nDid they come back to it within days,\n\n54\n00:02:17,662 --> 00:02:18,617\nor let it fade?\n\n55\n00:02:19,018 --> 00:02:20,017\nOr do they know it,\n\n56\n00:02:20,037 --> 00:02:22,320\nbut cannot find it without the right cue?\n\n57\n00:02:22,703 --> 00:02:24,917\nEach problem has a different fix.\n\n58\n00:02:25,087 --> 00:02:27,440\nEncode, consolidate, retrieve.\n\n59\n00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:29,837\nOver the next eleven episodes,\n\n60\n00:02:29,913 --> 00:02:32,395\nwe will follow one memory through all three.\n\n"
